.\" No version in the title line: see the note in irontile.1.
.TH IRONTILE-LOCK 1 "" "irontile" "irontile"
.SH NAME
irontile\-lock \- lock the session until a password says otherwise
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B irontile\-lock
.RB [ \-f | \-\-daemonize ]
.RB [ \-\-service
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR NAME ]
.br
.B irontile\-lock \-\-verify
.br
.B irontile\-lock \-\-dump
.I PATH
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR WxH ]
.RB [ \-\-scale
.IR N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B irontile\-lock
is an ordinary
.B ext\-session\-lock
client with no privileges of its own: it covers every display, asks PAM about a
password, and unlocks. Bind it, or hand it to an idle daemon.
.PP
PAM runs on a thread of its own, because it takes a couple of seconds to refuse
and that is deliberate: it is what makes guessing expensive. Asking it on the
loop that draws would stop the clock and the keyboard for that whole time, in
front of somebody who is already anxious about it.
.PP
The field shows a fixed number of pips and never grows. A track that grew as
the password was typed would tell anyone watching how long it is.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-service " NAME"
The file in
.I /etc/pam.d
to authenticate against. Default:
.BR irontile\-lock .
.TP
.BI \-\-user " NAME"
Who to authenticate as. Default:
.BR $USER .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-daemonize
Fork once the screen is actually locked, so that whatever started this can
carry on. An idle daemon told to lock before suspending waits for its lock
command to finish \(em that wait is the whole guarantee \(em and without this it
would wait until somebody unlocked, leaving a machine awake on a lock screen
with the lid shut.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Ask for a password on the terminal instead of locking anything, and say how
long PAM took to answer.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump " PATH"
Render one lock screen to a PNG and exit, without locking anything. The only
way to look at a lock screen while working on it.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " WxH"
What to render for
.BR \-\-dump .
Default 1692x1128.
.TP
.BI \-\-scale " N"
Display scale for
.BR \-\-dump .
Default 1.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a usage message and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/irontile\-lock
The PAM service this authenticates against. Installed by the package and left
alone on upgrade: what unlocking the screen requires is the administrator's to
decide. Without it nothing here will authenticate, which is the safe way round.
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/irontile\-lock\-fprint
A second service, asked on a thread of its own while the screen waits, so that
a finger and a password are two ways into the same lock and neither waits for
the other. Nothing installs this file and deleting it turns the reader off: a
lock screen that opens to a fingerprint is not something anybody should acquire
by upgrading. Put nothing in it but
.B pam_fprintd
and an account check \(em the fingerprint attempt is made with an empty
password, so a stack that falls through to the password database is a stack
being asked whether an empty password will do.
.SH EXAMPLES
Lock before suspending, and make the idle daemon wait for the screen to be
covered first:
.PP
.EX
swayidle \-w before\-sleep 'irontile\-lock \-f'
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irontile (1),
.BR pam (8),
.BR pam_fprintd (8)
